<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stairs Expedition to Katanga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Stairs Expedition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stairs Expedition was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> expedition of 1891–1892 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> led by the Canadian-born British officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-stairs">William Stairs</a>. Its aim was to secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-rich region before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a>, the capital of the Yeke kingdom, its officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omer-bodson">Omer Bodson</a> shot dead King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri</a> in December 1891. Stairs died of malaria on the return journey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stairs-expedition-to-katanga.jpg" alt="Stairs Expedition to Katanga" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Maloney, died 1896. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Captain_William_Stairs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
